From a9af0ca1ba19577c5e569c0e696c1ae88adb90ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: B Honey <9922368+Mhsbrian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] OPENBOOK: arm64: a compact record format for the translation
 cache
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Step P2b of the persistent translation cache (ANAL-381), on patch 12. A
Lichess lite record averaged 920 bytes for 402 bytes of code: the 24-byte
relocation entries were 41% of the cache, which put the app's lite set at
169 MB, over the 128 MiB cap. Format version 2 stores the same contents
with variable-length fields. Files of format 1 fail the header check and
their writer replaces them.

- A record is a 16-byte prefix (magic, size, CRC32C, tier, zero bytes),
  then canonical LEB128 varints and unpadded bytes: the address, end, sizes
  and counts; the guest bytes and code; each operand as an offset delta
  from the previous operand's end, a tag byte (kind, a 4-byte displacement,
  an explicit next_pc) and its argument, a guest offset or table slot
  relative to the region's address; recovery points by fault offset, as
  deltas.
- Index entries are 24 bytes, with 32-bit offsets. A batch that would put a
  record or its end past 4 GiB isn't written, rather than narrowed; file
  positions stay 64-bit.
- DecodeRecord checks the size and CRC first, on the private copy, then
  decodes with canonical, length-limited varints and checked arithmetic:
  truncated or overlong values, overflowing deltas, invalid tags, widths
  and flags, operands outside the code, recovery points outside it,
  counts the record can't hold (checked before anything grows) and
  trailing bytes all refuse the record. A displacement may only name a
  host entry or the translator image, a counter only appears in a counting
  region, and every guest address must lie in the record's module.
- RelinkTranslation refuses an image offset outside the translator image.
- Records are copied with memcpy, not range inserts, which measured about
  2 µs a record in the host build.
- The cap is described as best effort: a flush can write several modules'
  batches, and 1 MiB is when a flush starts, not a limit on it.

Tests: PersistentCacheFormat (4): varints at every size and their
non-canonical forms; a record with every symbol kind, both widths,
default and explicit next_pc, negative guest and recovery deltas, a
resource with an offset, every tier; what the encoder refuses; 29
malformed records and every truncation. PersistentCacheTest gains every
byte of a real record flipped (refused, or decoded and verified: the code
installed is always the fresh translation), records near and past 4 GiB in
a sparse file, and a format-1 file left alone by a reader and replaced by
its writer.

